{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological survival is descriptive and blind; scientific preference is normative and intentional.","Organisms do not choose to be tested; scientists actively design severe tests.","Evolution has no goal of 'truth'; science explicitly aims at truth (even if asymptotically)."],"overturn_conditions":["If the universe is shown to be non-uniform such that past severity guarantees nothing about future structure.","If it is proven that acting on corroboration inevitably requires an unconscious inductive leap."],"shared_structure":["Selection occurs based on past performance (survival/tests) without guarantee of future success.","The mechanism is retrospective (filtering failures) rather than prospective (predicting winners).","Preference for the 'fittest' or 'best-tested' is a heuristic for potential, not a proof of outcome."],"source_memory_refs":["biological_evolution_by_natural_selection"],"transfer_claims":["Just as we bet on the fittest organism without knowing the future environment, we bet on the best-corroborated theory without knowing the next test.","The rationality of the bet lies in the rigor of the filtering process, not in the probability of the winner."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms as a non-inductive, methodological rule of 'risk-taking' rather than a probabilistic inference, but accepting this defense costs the scientist any claim that their choice is 'likely true' or rationally superior in terms of expected utility, reducing scientific progress to a gamble on boldness without epistemic security.","counterconditions":["If the preference for corroborated theories implicitly assumes the future will resemble the past (uniformity of nature), then it smuggles in induction.","If 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) is required to justify the preference, and verisimilitude is logically incoherent for false theories (per Tichý/Miller), then the defense collapses.","If scientists act on corroboration because they believe the theory is 'more likely true,' then they are violating Popper's prohibition on probabilistic confirmation."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument reconstructs Popper's distinction between 'logical probability' (which remains low for highly testable theories) and 'methodological preference' (which favors severity). It posits that choosing the best-corroborated theory is akin to a gambler choosing the horse with the most rigorous past performance not because it will win (induction), but because it offers the highest potential payoff (truth-content) if it survives the next test. The mechanism relies on redefining 'rational action' as adherence to the rule of seeking falsification, not maximizing truth-probability.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}